Myth: Before the first morning opened its golden eye, the world lay thirsty and gray, and every village guarded its last clay cup. In one village lived Asha, a potter who shaped bowls so thin they rang when touched. She said the sound was the memory of rain.

One night, a silver moth landed on her kiln. “The rivers are not gone,” it whispered. “They are folded inside the Mountain of Clouds, waiting for a path made by gratitude.”

Asha asked each villager for one thing they could spare. A widow gave a button, a child gave a tear, a shepherd gave wool, and Asha gave a lock of her hair. She pressed them into wet clay and made a blue cup painted with feather marks.

Carrying it up the mountain, she met Thunder, who blocked the gate with a spear of fire. “Why should the sky open?” he roared. “Your people have forgotten thanks.”

Asha bowed and placed the empty cup before him. “Then let emptiness be our promise to receive gently.”

Thunder softened. He filled the cup with one bright drop. Asha poured it onto the dust below. The earth shivered, rivers unfolded, and rain began singing.

Since then, the first cup of water is always given back.
